  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "PS6_001_Collatz Path Printed Backwards",
   "display_name": "Collatz Path Printed Backwards",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Start with any positive integer <code>n</code> and repeat this rule: if the number is even, divide it by 2; if it is odd, multiply it by 3 and add 1. Keep going until you reach 1. For example, starting from 6 you visit:</p>\n<pre>6, 3, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1</pre>\n<p>Write a function <code>collatz_path(n)</code> that takes a positive integer (int &gt; 0) <code>n</code>, at most 1000, and <b>prints</b> this path <b>backwards</b>, from 1 back to <code>n</code>, all on one line, with the characters <code>&lt;-</code> between the numbers and no spaces anywhere. If <code>n</code> is 1, it prints just <code>1</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Rules for this question:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>You must simply use <b>recursion</b> (the function calls itself) and <b>print</b> inside the function. The function does not need to return anything.</li>\n<li><b>Do not</b> save the numbers anywhere temporarily (such as in a list) to reverse them later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Python fact you may use:</i> normally each <code>print()</code> starts a new line. <code>print(x, end=\"\")</code> prints <code>x</code> and stays on the same line, so the next print continues right after it. For example, <code>print(\"ab\", end=\"\")</code> followed by <code>print(\"cd\", end=\"\")</code> shows <code>abcd</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>collatz_path(6)</code> prints <code>1&lt;-2&lt;-4&lt;-8&lt;-16&lt;-5&lt;-10&lt;-3&lt;-6</code></li>\n<li><code>collatz_path(1)</code> prints <code>1</code></li>\n<li><code>collatz_path(2)</code> prints <code>1&lt;-2</code></li>\n<li><code>collatz_path(5)</code> prints <code>1&lt;-2&lt;-4&lt;-8&lt;-16&lt;-5</code></li>\n</ul>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
