  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "PS6_004_Stone Game Winner",
   "display_name": "Stone Game Winner",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Harry and Ron play a game with a pile of <code>n</code> stones. They take turns, and <b>Harry always moves first</b>. On each turn, a player must remove <b>1, 2 or 3</b> stones from the pile (not more than the stones left). The player who removes the <b>last stone wins</b>.</p>\n<p>Both players are very clever and always play perfectly: whenever a player has a move that guarantees a win no matter what the other player does later, they take it.</p>\n<p>Some small cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>n = 1, 2 or 3: Harry takes all the stones at once and wins.</li>\n<li>n = 4: whether Harry takes 1, 2 or 3 stones, Ron is left with 3, 2 or 1 stones and takes them all. So Ron wins.</li>\n<li>n = 5: Harry takes 1 stone, leaving 4 for Ron. Now Ron is in the same bad situation Harry was in with 4 stones, so Harry wins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write a function <code>winner(n)</code> that takes a positive integer (int &gt; 0) <code>n</code>, at most 15, and <b>returns</b> the string <code>\"Harry\"</code> or <code>\"Ron\"</code>: the name of the player who wins when both play perfectly.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> <code>winner(1)</code> returns <code>\"Harry\"</code>. <code>winner(4)</code> returns <code>\"Ron\"</code>. <code>winner(5)</code> returns <code>\"Harry\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
