  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "PS6_005_Look And Say",
   "display_name": "Look And Say",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>look-and-say</b> game works like this: you read a string of digits aloud from left to right, describing each group of <b>consecutive equal digits</b> by saying how many there are, followed by the digit. Writing down what you said gives the next string.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>\"1211\"</code> is read as \"one 1, one 2, two 1s\", which is written <code>\"111221\"</code>. And <code>\"3322251\"</code> is read as \"two 3s, three 2s, one 5, one 1\", which is written <code>\"23321511\"</code>.</p>\n<p>The count is written in full, so a group of ten 1s (<code>\"1111111111\"</code>) becomes <code>\"101\"</code> (ten, then 1).</p>\n<p>Write a function <code>look_and_say(s)</code> that takes a non-empty string <code>s</code> made only of digit characters, and <b>returns</b> the next string in the look-and-say game, as a string.</p><p><i>Python facts you may use:</i> a string can be indexed just like a list, so if <code>s = \"hello\"</code> then <code>s[0]</code> is <code>\"h\"</code>, <code>s[4]</code> is <code>\"o\"</code>, and <code>len(s)</code> is 5. You can build up a new string step by step with <code>+</code>, and <code>str(3)</code> gives the string <code>\"3\"</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>look_and_say(\"1\")</code> returns <code>\"11\"</code></li>\n<li><code>look_and_say(\"21\")</code> returns <code>\"1211\"</code></li>\n<li><code>look_and_say(\"1211\")</code> returns <code>\"111221\"</code></li>\n<li><code>look_and_say(\"3322251\")</code> returns <code>\"23321511\"</code></li>\n</ul>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
