  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "PS6_006_Print In Binary",
   "display_name": "Print In Binary",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Computers store numbers in <b>binary</b>. In binary, numbers are written using only the digits <b>0</b> and <b>1</b>. Each place is worth <b>double</b> the place to its right: counting from the right, the places are worth 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and so on. To find the value of a binary number, add up the place values that have a 1.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><th>Number</th><th>Binary</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>101</td><td>4 + 0 + 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>110</td><td>4 + 2 + 0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>1000</td><td>8 + 0 + 0 + 0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>1101</td><td>8 + 4 + 0 + 1</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>A clue:</b> the <b>last</b> binary digit of a number tells you whether the number is even or odd (it is 0 for even numbers and 1 for odd numbers).</p>\n<p>Write a function <code>print_binary(n)</code> that takes a non-negative integer (int &gt;= 0) <code>n</code>, at most 100000, and <b>prints</b> its binary representation on one line (with no extra zeros at the start).</p>\n<p><b>Rules for this question:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>You must simply use <b>recursion</b> (the function calls itself) and <b>print</b> inside the function. The function does not need to return anything.</li>\n<li><b>Do not</b> save the digits anywhere temporarily (such as in a list) to reverse them later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Python fact you may use:</i> normally each <code>print()</code> starts a new line. <code>print(x, end=\"\")</code> prints <code>x</code> and stays on the same line, so the next print continues right after it. For example, <code>print(\"ab\", end=\"\")</code> followed by <code>print(\"cd\", end=\"\")</code> shows <code>abcd</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> <code>print_binary(13)</code> prints <code>1101</code>. <code>print_binary(8)</code> prints <code>1000</code>. <code>print_binary(0)</code> prints <code>0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
