  {
   "xml_index": 7,
   "xml_name": "PS6_007_Choosing A Crew",
   "display_name": "Choosing A Crew",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Captain Luffy has <code>n</code> crew members and must choose exactly <code>k</code> of them for a secret mission. Only <b>who</b> is chosen matters, not the order in which they are chosen. So choosing Zoro and Nami is the same choice as choosing Nami and Zoro.</p>\n<p>For example, with 4 crew members (Zoro, Nami, Usopp, Sanji) and k = 2, there are 6 different choices:</p>\n<pre>Zoro + Nami\nZoro + Usopp\nZoro + Sanji\nNami + Usopp\nNami + Sanji\nUsopp + Sanji</pre>\n<p>Write a function <code>choose_crew(n, k)</code> that takes two non-negative integers <code>n</code> and <code>k</code>, with <code>k &lt;= n</code> and <code>n</code> at most 20, and <b>returns</b> the number of different ways to choose <code>k</code> crew members out of <code>n</code>. Choosing 0 people (or choosing everybody) can be done in exactly 1 way.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> <code>choose_crew(4, 2)</code> returns <code>6</code>. <code>choose_crew(5, 3)</code> returns <code>10</code>. <code>choose_crew(5, 0)</code> returns <code>1</code>. <code>choose_crew(5, 5)</code> returns <code>1</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(choose_crew(4, 2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "6",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(choose_crew(5, 3))",
