   "xml_index": 8,
   "xml_name": "PS6_008_Nested List Max",
   "display_name": "Nested List Max",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <b>nested list</b> is a list whose elements can be integers <b>or other lists</b>, and those inner lists can again contain integers or lists, to any depth. For example:</p>\n<pre>[3, [8, [1, 9]], 2]</pre>\n<p>Here the elements are: the integer 3, the list [8, [1, 9]] (which itself contains the list [1, 9]), and the integer 2.</p>\n<p>Write a function <code>nested_max(lst)</code> that takes such a nested list and <b>returns</b> the <b>largest</b> integer found anywhere inside it, at any level.</p>\n<p>You can assume that <b>no list is empty</b>, at any level, so every list contains at least one integer somewhere inside it. The integers may be negative.</p>\n<p><i>Python fact you may use:</i> <code>type(x) == list</code> is <code>True</code> if <code>x</code> is a list, and <code>False</code> if <code>x</code> is an integer.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>nested_max([3, [8, [1, 9]], 2])</code> returns <code>9</code></li>\n<li><code>nested_max([1, [2, 30], [[4]], 5])</code> returns <code>30</code></li>\n<li><code>nested_max([[[7]]])</code> returns <code>7</code></li>\n<li><code>nested_max([-5, [-2, [-9]]])</code> returns <code>-2</code></li>\n</ul>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(nested_max([3, [8, [1, 9]], 2]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "9",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
