   "xml_name": "PS6_010_Decrypt The Secret Message",
   "display_name": "Decrypt The Secret Message",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In the Hogwarts library, you find an old piece of paper with this secret message:</p>\n<pre>pa kvlz uva kv av kdlss vu kylhtz huk mvynla av spcl</pre>\n<p>A note next to it explains how the message was made: every letter of the original message was replaced by the letter a fixed number of places <b>later</b> in the alphabet. If counting forward goes past <code>z</code>, it continues from <code>a</code> again. Spaces were not changed. The note does not say what the fixed number is.</p>\n<p>Someone before you has already worked out that the first word, <code>pa</code>, decodes to <code>it</code>.</p>\n<p>Write a function <code>decrypt(text)</code> that takes any message encrypted in this same way (with the same fixed number), and <b>returns</b> the original message. You can assume <code>text</code> contains only lowercase letters and spaces.</p><p><i>Python facts you may use:</i> a string can be indexed just like a list, so if <code>s = \"hello\"</code> then <code>s[0]</code> is <code>\"h\"</code>, <code>s[4]</code> is <code>\"o\"</code>, and <code>len(s)</code> is 5. You may also use the string <code>\"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\"</code> in your code. You can build up a new string step by step with <code>+</code>, and <code>str(3)</code> gives the string <code>\"3\"</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>decrypt(\"pa kvlz uva kv av kdlss vu kylhtz huk mvynla av spcl\")</code> returns the original message, which is a well-known quote. Your function must also work on other messages encrypted the same way.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
